-- #1513, the OTHER half of the same property (found while fixing it; MEASURED).
--
-- Both emit backends store a record cell POSITIONALLY, in the order the `CRecord`
-- node lists its fields, while every reader indexes by the DECLARED order.  Their
-- in-source comments already asserted the two coincide -- wasm_emit's
-- `emitRecordRef` says "CRecord carries fields in DECLARED order (lowering
-- preserves it)" -- but nothing established it.  No pass reordered a literal, and
-- the field-order table was whatever the FIRST construction site happened to write.
--
-- So `a` below, written `{ g = …, f = … }`, installed `[g, f]` as the layout of
-- `R`, and `b`, written in declared order, was laid out `[f, g]` and read with
-- `a`'s indices.  MEASURED on `main` @ 0675ce16 before the fix: `medaka run`
-- printed the correct `10 30` and the BUILT BINARY printed `10 40` at exit 0.
--
-- `core_ir_lower.normalizeRecordOrder` now makes the asserted invariant true, at
-- the one point every emit driver funnels through, so both backends inherit it.
-- The field expressions are bound to temporaries IN WRITTEN ORDER first, so an
-- effectful field expression still runs when the source says it does.
--
-- This is also the shape issue #1306 pinned (its fixture drained on this fix and is
-- removed with it), so the last component reads the field back through a RECORD
-- PATTERN rather than a projection -- #1306's own spelling.  Both go through the
-- same positional layout, and keeping both means the fixture still fails if only
-- one of the two readers is repaired.
--
-- EXPECTED VALUE, HAND-DERIVED from this source before any engine was run --
-- pinned at test/engine_value_pins/engine/record_literal_field_order.pin:
--
--   (10, 20, 30, 40, 10)
--
--   `a` is `{ g = 20, f = 10 }`, so `a.f = 10` and `a.g = 20`.
--   `b` is `{ f = 30, g = 40 }`, so `b.f = 30` and `b.g = 40`.
--   the last `10` is `a`'s `f` again, read by pattern instead of by projection.
--
-- `b` is the BYSTANDER: it is written in declared order, so it was laid out
-- correctly by the OLD rule as well -- for every program where no sibling literal
-- disagreed.  It must still be correct now that the declaration, not `a`, decides.
-- `40` in the third slot instead of `30` is the defect's signature.

data R = R { f : Int, g : Int }

getF : R -> Int
getF r = r.f

getG : R -> Int
getG r = r.g

main =
  let a = R { g = 20, f = 10 }
  let b = R { f = 30, g = 40 }
  let patF = match a
    R { f } => f
  (getF a, getG a, getF b, getG b, patF)
